hance uh hdr  and so  uh i'll have a portion of my talk that  focuses on using small molecules to  modulate repair pathways  and then a section looking at our  current uh and future  products  where we've added modifications into  repair templates for improved stability  and better hdr  and we're very excited to talk about  those  all right so uh we do have a full  workflow for uh crispr research at idt  going all the way from experimental  design through analysis  uh and i'm not gonna cover all of this  uh but  uh in my talk i will be  discussing our hdr design tool which has  a friendly ui and  provides optimal  donor templates  based on empirically defined design  rules  and that is fully integrated with our  cas9 guide rna design tool as well  we also have a full suite of  guide rnas and crispr proteins available  i won't be focusing specifically on  those but  for our hdr reagents i will be covering  our donor templates  and so  for  short edits we have our altar hdr donor  oligos that are available up to 200  bases  and those are modified  single-stranded oligos based on our  ultramark chemistry  the  for longer  templates we offer currently our megamer  long single-stranded dnas up to 2000  bases  but we are very excited uh that we'll be  releasing our altar hdr donor blocks  which are modified linear double  stranded dnas that are up to 3000 base  pairs  and those modifications are designed to  increase hdr and also reduce  potential off-target effects  and so those will be available  later this year or early next year  and the final product that i'll be  highlighting is our  small molecule  hdr enhancers um  and actually that should be updated our  version two just launched this month um  all right so now getting into the data  the first step in designing any hdr  experiment  is guide selection and there are three  big factors to consider for that  the first is the position of the  cleavage site  generally you want to be  as close  to the hdr insertion as p