ocess with our hdr design tool  and on this first menu you'll be  selecting your target  and there is a very flexible input  format  that includes either selecting a gene or  genomic coordinates for  common genomes including human mouse  rats c elegans and zebrafish  and if you're using one of these common  genomes it does allow you to select an  ncbi transcript  and so that will automatically upload  coding information  uh for incorporating silent mutations in  the subsequent menus  if you're working in other species or  using batch designs we also offer a  fafsa input  and further down on the menu you can  also enter in the edit location  if you know exactly where you want to do  your edit  and  you can enter in guide information as  well if you've already confirmed those  elements  otherwise they can be selected on the  interactive interface later  all right uh so on the second menu here  uh after you've picked your targets um  you can then select your specific hdr  edit  and so if you entered in uh the  coordinates for your hdr edit it will  automatically zoom in otherwise you can  use these blue sliders here  to select the uh specific location that  you want to introduce a sequence  or a mutation  and so  uh if you want to generate a snip or a  deletion you can select  the bases that you want to edit  and then enter in the replacement down  below here on the mutation bar  otherwise you can  put  push these two sliders together  and  any sequence that you enter in the  mutation bar will be entered in  as in uh direct insert  wherever those blue sliders are set  and you can see  on the little map  if you have entered in your ncbi  transcript it does give you the  translation of the gene down below  and above you can see that it is entered  in that hdr edit that we have typed in  additionally you can select  specific homology arm lengths we do have  defaults of 40 for short edits and  200 for larger edits  and down below you can also select  whether you want the tool to  automatically incorporate blocking  mutations  all right  so this is what the design output looks  like from the tool and so in this  particular example uh we're showing two  different outputs  on the top you can see it is the output  without any blocking mutations  incorporated  and so you can see the guide rna that  we've selected is shown  down below as well as the cut site is  indicated  uh the hdr edit is highlighted in  green  and you can see the translations down  below so we can confirm that's correct  as well  and in the second panel down below you  can see  uh  indicated where those two specific  blocking mutations are  and on the right we do have  some hdr data here  looking for that  perfect hdr in dark blue  and you can see that those silent  mutations do indeed increase the hdr  rate  and this tool is fully integrated with  our  web ordering so it will allow you to  order the paired guide rna and the  hdr template as well  all right uh so moving beyond our hdr  design tool uh unfortunately you may run  into a situation  where guide availability is limited  in your sequence of interest  and as most of you are likely aware hdr  efficiency does really drop off after  you get beyond uh 10 or 15 base pairs  away from the hdr edit  and cut site  and so an example of that is just shown  here where we've walked uh the hdr  insert  position uh for a six space insert  um  and so we asked uh how can we improve  the hdr rates  in these cases where you're cutting uh  over uh 15 base pairs away from the cut  site  and so one important factor that we have  found with these types of edits  is strand selection  and so when you're using a single  stranded oligo  like in these experiments  we have typically found  that  hdr edits that are near the cut site  don't have any generalized  strand preference so we empirically  determine  whether the non-targeting or targeting  strand would be preferred  however  in this case we're introducing  again a six base insert  