n the  same side of the double stranded break  as the hdr edit  all right  so now moving on beyond the improved  designs  the next portion of the talk will focus  on improved reagents  and so we'll start out first  discussing our small molecules to  modulate repair pathways  and then focus in on the  modified  donor oligos and donor blocks  for improved hdr  so  for the small molecule enhancers  um  our altar hdr enhancer v2 has really  been a game-changing improvement for us  for hdr rates  so after launching our original hdr  enhancer  which is shown here in light orange  which gives about a four-fold increase  in hdr in this experiment  we found that  it was  better than the majority of the  compounds that were published in the  literature and you can show or see some  of our screening results here  and what we found when we looked at  compounds from the literature is that  the majority of them really didn't have  a strong impact on our hdr rates  however after we launched our original  hdr enhancer we did continue evaluating  new compounds  and our  best new solution  that offers  improved hdr is shown here in dark  orange which is our recently launched  altar hdr enhancer v2  and you can  see here that we're getting a cherries  up to eighty percent for this six space  insert in jerket cells  and in addition to  better hdr efficiency uh the v2 enhancer  can also be delivered at a lower dose  and so that allows for better style  viability as well  okay  and on this slide i'm just showing  various  uh small edits that we've made uh in  varying human cell lines as well as  varying milestone lines  and you can see across the board that we  get increased hdr  when using either our  version one or version two enhancers uh  but that the  version two offers the best hdr around  um  and uh i don't have the data shown here  uh but we've also shown that it works  well for improving caswell by hdr as  well  all right so now moving into modified  templates  for  repair template sequences that are less  than 200 bases  our hdr design tool will automatically  design ultra hdr donor oligos  and so  again these  short oligos are based on our top  quality altimeters  and these include additional  modifications that help boost hdr and so  this modification pattern was  the result of extensive testing  with oligos to identify a top  modification pattern to improve hdrs and  so here you can see um  is a summary of our testing  and so this was tested at multiple sites  and in multiple human and male cell  lines and you can see  we get about a seven-fold improvement  on average over an unmodified uh oligo  and we're also highlighting here  um that phosphorothyroid modified donors  which have been published in the  literature give about a four-fold uh  increase in hdr relative to the  unmodified so  the increased stability that you get  in cells  with the donor also is beneficial for  improved hdr as  well and on the slide is just a little  bit more  direct comparison  at individual sites  in our human jerk at cell line and in  the mouse cell line h3612j  and again this is a  short six-space insert that's being  generated in each of these cases  and you can see  if we look at the unmodified donor we're  starting at about one to two percent  hdr in these challenging systems  we do get a decent boost to the hdra  when using those phosphorothyroid  modified donors but we get much better  hdr with the altar modified  donor all logos as well  all right now finally for this section  i do want to highlight that the combined  use of the altar hdr donor oligos and  the altar hdr enhancer v2 offers an  improved solution for generating those  small hdr edits particularly when you're  in a challenging system and starting off  at one or two percent hdr  and so  uh  just starting with our baseline of  unmodified untreated uh  uh reagents you can see in the dark blue  that we're starting between one to ten  percent hdr  um  and if we add the  hdr enhancer v2 we do get a nice uh  boost to the hdr rates and as we add  further modifications to the donors you  can see again we're getting a nice boost  to the hdr rates  uh and with our combined approach you  can see we're getting above 40 percent  uh hdr in all of these cases  all right  so now shifting gears to discuss larger  napkins  and go over some work from  the development of one of our upcoming  products  when we considered potential repair  templates for larger napkins  one of the easiest to produce is plasmid  dna  however we found  in our testing that the hdr efficiency  is limited when using plasmid  and that's because it can have higher  toxicity with your cells which  eliminates or limits the effective dose  that you can deliver  and so for  many years now we've offered our mega  mer long single stranded dna product  and this works well for hdr  and  is pretty well tolerated by cells  however it is a fairly complicated  manufacturing process and so  it does have a higher cost and  turnaround time  and so this has  generally worked well for  my micro injection work uh the yields  that are generated are sufficient for  that  however it's not really a cost of  effective a cost effective solution for  cell work  and so we started looking into  alternatives and turned to linear  double-stranded dna  and generally linear double-stranded dna  we found does work well for hdr  and has similar toxicity  to cells  as long single-stranded dna  however its main drawback is the risk of  off target integration which i'll get  into on the next slide  and so  we decided based on reports in the  literature to see if we could  incorporate modifications into the  linear double-stranded dna  to help mitigate this risk and promote  hdr  all right  so when we're  referring to this off-target risk  what that really comes down to is  homology independent integration with  the linear double-stranded dna  and so uh after the double-stranded dna  is uh generated ideally when you  introduce 