hance uh hdr  and so  uh i'll have a portion of my talk that  focuses on using small molecules to  modulate repair pathways  and then a section looking at our  current uh and future  products  where we've added modifications into  repair templates for improved stability  and better hdr  and we're very excited to talk about  those  all right so uh we do have a full  workflow for uh crispr research at idt  going all the way from experimental  design through analysis  uh and i'm not gonna cover all of this  uh but  uh in my talk i will be  discussing our hdr design tool which has  a friendly ui and  provides optimal  donor templates  based on empirically defined design  rules  and that is fully integrated with our  cas9 guide rna design tool as well  we also have a full suite of  guide rnas and crispr proteins available  i won't be focusing specifically on  those but  for our hdr reagents i will be covering  our donor templates  and so  for  short edits we have our altar hdr donor  oligos that are available up to 200  bases  and those are modified  single-stranded oligos based on our  ultramark chemistry  the  for longer  templates we offer currently our megamer  long single-stranded dnas up to 2000  bases  but we are very excited uh that we'll be  releasing our altar hdr donor blocks  which are modified linear double  stranded dnas that are up to 3000 base  pairs  and those modifications are designed to  increase hdr and also reduce  potential off-target effects  and so those will be available  later this year or early next year  and the final product that i'll be  highlighting is our  small molecule  hdr enhancers um  and actually that should be updated our  version two just launched this month um  all right so now getting into the data  the first step in designing any hdr  experiment  is guide selection and there are three  big factors to consider for that  the first is the position of the  cleavage site  generally you want to be  as close  to the hdr insertion as possible  the second point is guide efficiency and  generally you need to have high  efficiency to mediate high levels of hdr  and if you  are using a poor efficiency guide that's  really going to  impact negatively  your potential hdr rate  and the last factor to keep in mind is  the risk for off-target effects  and so we recommend using a guide with a  good specificity score um either using  our guide rna tool or others available  to predict off targets  and that'll just help reduce any  potential off-target activity  and so in the next couple slides uh  i'll be  uh showing a case study to highlight the  importance of this second point looking  for guide efficiency  um and actually confirming your guide  efficiency prior to hdr experiments so  in this  experiment we are targeting a six base  pair insert  to the gap dh gene uh right before the  stop codon  and so we're considering a number of  factors here again  the position of that cleavage site  relative to the hdr edit  shown here are  a number of guides that cut between 2 to  14 base pairs away we did test further  out as well  and then  the other factor that we're considering  here is guide efficiency  and so if we look at the actual hdr  rates for a number of these guides  targeting this locus what we found is  that the two closest guides  here that cut two and five base pairs  away  actually turned out to have very poor  total editing which you can see here  measured by t7e1  and so because of that low overall  editing efficiency with these two guides  you can see that the hdr rates are  correspondingly  limited  and actually the best guide that we  found to work at this site  it cuts six-pace pairs downstream  from our target insert site and you can  see we can get hdr rates uh up to 50  percent as measured by equal r1's uh  cleavage  uh  and what we found surprising is actually  the second best guide uh cuts 14 base  pairs away  upstream  and again that's a high efficiency guide  and so we were able to get higher rates  of hdr  so this is an extreme case where the  editing rates are lower but it does  highlight an important  step of confirming your editing  efficiency with your guides prior to hdr  experiments  all right so the introduction of  blocking mutations into the hdr donor is  particularly useful when the hdr edit  does not disrupt caslin recognition and  so for building out our altar hdr design  tool we set out to build a empirically  defined rule set  to base our recommendations on and so  i'm highlighting here a sample  experiment from that testing  and so for this experiment we were  introducing a snip adjacent to the pam  where the hdr event is susceptible to  further editing by cast9  and then we assessed whether a single  blocking mutation  was sufficient to prevent cast 9  recutting and give us perfect hdr  and  to do that we mutated each position  within the pam and within the guide seed  region to every other possible base  and as you can see we tested this in  two cell lines and at two different  sites and the results here are plotted  as a heat map  and what we found is that uh introducing  blocking mutations within the g  positions of the pam or neither the  three prime end of the guide  uh brought our hdr rates  up from around one percent as a baseline  up to 10 to 20 hdr  and we also found that no base was  universally preferred over others  and as i indicated we did do further  testing looking at combinations of  mutations as well  but that all was built into the  rule set for our design tool and up in  the upper right you can see after  developing this rule set we then did  check  the tools model for  blocking mutations with a variety of  edits  including various size insertions at  different sites as well as snips and  deletions  and aside from one data point  you can see that the hdr8 benefited from  the addition of these silent mutations  from our design tool  all right so now on the next few slides  i'll be running through the design  process with our hdr design tool  and on this first menu you'll be  selecting your target  and there is a very flexible input  format  that includes either selecting a gene or  genomic coordinates for  common genomes including human mouse  rats c elegans and zebrafish  and if you're using one of these common  genomes it does allow you to select an  ncbi transcript  and so that will automatically upload  coding information  uh for incorporating silent mutations in  the subsequent menus  if you're working in other species or  using batch designs we also offer a  fafsa input  and further down on the menu you can  also enter in the edit location  if you know exactly where you want to do  your edit  and  you can enter in guide information as  well if you've already confirmed those  elements  otherwise they can be selected on the  interactive interface later  all right uh so on the second menu here  uh after you've picked your targets um  you can then select your specific hdr  edit  and so if you entered in uh the  coordinates for your hdr edit it will  automatically zoom in otherwise you can  use these blue sliders here  to select the uh specific location that  you want to introduce a sequence  or a mutation  and so  uh if you want to generate a snip or a  deletion you can select  the bases that you want to edit  and then enter in the replacement down  below here on the mutation bar  otherwise you can  put  push these two sliders together  and  any sequence that you enter in the  mutation bar will be entered in  as in uh direct insert  wherever those blue sliders are set  and you can see  on the little map  if you have entered in your ncbi  transcript it does give you the  translation of the gene down below  and above you can see that it is entered  in that hdr edit that we have typed in  additionally you can select  specific homology arm lengths we do have  defaults of 40 for short edits and  200 for larger edits  and down below you can also select  whether you want the tool to  automatically incorporate blocking  mutations  all right  so this is what the design output looks  like from the tool and so in this  particular example uh we're showing two  different outputs  on the top you can see it is the output  without any blocking mutations  incorporated  and so you can see the guide rna that  we've selected is shown  down below as well as the cut site is  indicated  uh the hdr edit is highlighted in  green  and you can see the translations down  below so we can confirm that's correct  as well  and in the second panel down below you  can see  uh  indicated where those two specific  blocking mutations are  and on the right we do have  some hdr data here  looking for that  perfect hdr in dark blue  and you can see that those silent  mutations do indeed increase the hdr  rate  and this tool is fully integrated with  our  web ordering so it will allow you to  order the paired guide rna and the  hdr template as well  all right uh so moving beyond our hdr  design tool uh unfortunately you may run  into a situation  where guide availability is limited  in your sequence of interest  and as most of you are likely aware hdr  efficiency does really drop off after  you get beyond uh 10 or 15 base pairs  away from the hdr edit  and cut site  and so an example of that is just shown  here where we've walked uh the hdr  insert  position uh for a six space insert  um  and so we asked uh how can we improve  the hdr rates  in these cases where you're cutting uh  over uh 15 base pairs away from the cut  site  and so one important factor that we have  found with these types of edits  is strand selection  and so when you're using a single  stranded oligo  like in these experiments  we have typically found  that  hdr edits that are near the cut site  don't have any generalized  strand preference so we empirically  determine  whether the non-targeting or targeting  strand would be preferred  however  in this case we're introducing  again a six base insert  20 base pairs pam distal or pam proximal  relative to our guide sequence  and in these cases uh for these two  sites tested here that were pulled from  a larger testing set for simplicity you  can see that  pam distal edits have a very strong  preference for the non-targeting strand  uh  while the edits that are placed paint  proximal have a very strong preference  for the targeting strand  so if you're using single stranded  oligos  and editing far away from your cas9  cleavage site this is an important  factor to keep in mind  the  we then further investigated whether or  not there were any preferences for  the placement of silent blocking  mutations for these types of hdr edits  and so again  in this experiment we were  inserting a six base sequence  in this case 25 base pairs away from the  cup site  and looking at it at that  with both pam distal edits uh and with  pam proximal edits  and then we tested donors that either  had no further mutation  included a pain mutation or included a  repair track mutation and so  we just evenly spaced out  snips between the cut site and the  hdr insert  and so what we found if we look at the  preferred strand in both of these cases  is that  mutations that are placed on the same  side of the double-stranded break as the  hdr edit were preferred  and so that particularly comes into play  with that pam distal edit  so if we look at these  hdr8 by ngs you can see that we don't  have any perfect hdr  when no extra mutation is  incorporated the pam mutation does bring  that up to about four percent uh on the  pam distal side  um at the three low side we tested this  at  um  however if we then included those repair  track mutations uh where those snips  were placed on the same side of the  double strand of break as our hdr edit  you can see we're bringing that up to  about 13  in the case of the pam proximal  mutations  there wasn't any really further benefit  to the repair track mutations over the  pam mutation since that's all on the  same side of the double stranded break  as the hdr edit  all right  so now moving on beyond the improved  designs  the next portion of the talk will focus  on improved reagents  and so we'll start out first  discussing our small molecules to  modulate repair pathways  and then focus in on the  modified  donor oligos and donor blocks  for improved hdr  so  for the small molecule enhancers  um  our altar hdr enhancer v2 has really  been a game-changing improvement for us  for hdr rates  so after launching our original hdr  enhancer  which is shown here in light orange  which gives about a four-fold increase  in hdr in this experiment  we found that  it was  better than the majority of the  compounds that were published in the  literature and you can show or see some  of our screening results here  and what we found when we looked at  compounds from the literature is that  the majority of them really didn't have  a strong impact on our hdr rates  however after we launched our original  hdr enhancer we did continue evaluating  new compounds  and our  best new solution  that offers  improved hdr is shown here in dark  orange which is our recently launched  altar hdr enhancer v2  and you can  see here that we're getting a cherries  up to eighty percent for this six space  insert in jerket cells  and in addition to  better hdr efficiency uh the v2 enhancer  can also be delivered at a lower dose  and so that allows for better style  viability as well  okay  and on this slide i'm just showing  various  uh small edits that we've made uh in  varying human cell lines as well as  varying milestone lines  and you can see across the board that we  get increased hdr  when using either our  version one or version two enhancers uh  but that the  version two offers the best hdr around  um  and uh i don't have the data shown here  uh but we've also shown that it works  well for improving caswell by hdr as  well  all right so now moving into modified  templates  for  repair template sequences that are less  than 200 bases  our hdr design tool will automatically  design ultra hdr donor oligos  and so  again these  short oligos are based on our top  quality altimeters  and these include additional  modifications that help boost hdr and so  this modification pattern was  the result of extensive testing  with oligos to identify a top  modification pattern to improve hdrs and  so here you can see um  is a summary of our testing  and so this was tested at multiple sites  and in multiple human and male cell  lines and you can see  we get about a seven-fold improvement  on average over an unmodified uh oligo  and we're also highlighting here  um that phosphorothyroid modified donors  which have been published in the  literature give about a four-fold uh  increase in hdr relative to the  unmodified so  the increased stability that you get  in cells  with the donor also is beneficial for  improved hdr as  well and on the slide is just a little  bit more  direct comparison  at individual sites  in our human jerk at cell line and in  the mouse cell line h3612j  and again this is a  short six-space insert that's being  generated in each of these cases  and you can see  if we look at the unmodified donor we're  starting at about one to two percent  hdr in these challenging systems  we do get a decent boost to the hdra  when using those phosphorothyroid  modified donors but we get much better  hdr with the altar modified  donor all logos as well  all right now finally for this section  i do want to highlight that the combined  use of the altar hdr donor oligos and  the altar hdr enhancer v2 offers an  improved solution for generating those  small hdr edits particularly when you're  in a challenging system and starting off  at one or two percent hdr  and so  uh  just starting with our baseline of  unmodified untreated uh  uh reagents you can see in the dark blue  that we're starting between one to ten  percent hdr  um  and if we add the  hdr enhancer v2 we do get a nice uh  boost to the hdr rates and as we add  further modifications to the donors you  can see again we're getting a nice boost  to the hdr rates  uh and with our combined approach you  can see we're getting above 40 percent  uh hdr in all of these cases  all right  so now shifting gears to discuss larger  napkins  and go over some work from  the development of one of our upcoming  products  when we considered potential repair  templates for larger napkins  one of the easiest to produce is plasmid  dna  however we found  in our testing that the hdr efficiency  is limited when using plasmid  and that's because it can have higher  toxicity with your cells which  eliminates or limits the effective dose  that you can deliver  and so for  many years now we've offered our mega  mer long single stranded dna product  and this works well for hdr  and  is pretty well tolerated by cells  however it is a fairly complicated  manufacturing process and so  it does have a higher cost and  turnaround time  and so this has  generally worked well for  my micro injection work uh the yields  that are generated are sufficient for  that  however it's not really a cost of  effective a cost effective solution for  cell work  and so we started looking into  alternatives and turned to linear  double-stranded dna  and generally linear double-stranded dna  we found does work well for hdr  and has similar toxicity  to cells  as long single-stranded dna  however its main drawback is the risk of  off target integration which i'll get  into on the next slide  and so  we decided based on reports in the  literature to see if we could  incorporate modifications into the  linear double-stranded dna  to help mitigate this risk and promote  hdr  all right  so when we're  referring to this off-target risk  what that really comes down to is  homology independent integration with  the linear double-stranded dna  and so uh after the double-stranded dna  is uh generated ideally when you  introduce your long template  you'll get a nice clean repair through  hdr  however the linear double-stranded dna  ends can similarly be recognized by the  nhj repair machinery and so you can get  insertion through the nhj pathway  and so if you're looking at your  ontarget site that can result in the  duplication of one or both of your  homology arms  and this can similarly happen  at any endogenous double-stranded dna  breaks  or cast 9 off target sites  and so the on target site isn't as much  of a concern since you can screen out  those edits fairly easily  however those  integration into any off target sites is  a concern  all right so we set out to screen a wide  variety of modifications  for the ability to prevent that off  target  integration and so here i'm showing you  a sample of that screening results  so generally our system involved  introducing modifications into a short  double-stranded dna donor  in this case uh inserting a 42 base pair  insert  that contained an ecor1 restriction site  we would then deliver this donor with a  cas9 that was complex with either the  on-target guide rna  or a guide rna targeting another locus  which gave us an opportunity to  artificially look at  off-target double-stranded dna breaks so  after editing we would then amplify up  these target sites  and assess insertion using equal or one  digestion and then based on the sizes of  the digestion products we could  determine whether it was hdr  or a blunt insertion  and so in general we selected  modifications such as g  shown here where we got an increase in  the hdr rates  as well as a decrease in the blunt  insertion rates  at both the on and off target  sites  all right so after we had done all of  our screening  we then set out to further confirm them  in additional cell lines and at  additional sites and a summary of our  results is shown here  and so when we were testing with these  shorter sequences we found that our top  modification pattern  labeled here as the altar  mod  offered improved  ratios of hdr to blunt insertion at the  on target site  up to four fold  alright so once we had identified our  modification  we then set out to look at how it  behaved with large knock-ins relative to  alternative templates and so here we're  comparing  long single-stranded dna  either the targeting or non-targeting  strands  relative to an unmodified  double-stranded dna or our altar  modified double stranded dna and you can  see  when we target a gfp insert three  different loci  in two different cell lines  you can see our baseline hdra with the  long single stranded dnas  was  less than 5  if we delivered an equal molar dose of  the unmodified double stranded dna you  can see that gets us up to about 10  um  and  if we then used our altar modified  double stranded dna that brought us up  to about 15 hdr so we're seeing improved  rates um  and if we then introduced our alter hdr  enhancer v2 what we found uh  was very pleasing that the treatment of  the v2 enhancer works very well with  linear double stranded dna  and so you can see that the v2 brings  the single stranded dna templates up to  10 to 20  hdr  uh however  we get up to 50 to 70 percent  hdr with the  v2 enhancer and the hdr donor blocks  and if we use long-range sequencing to  further dig into the editing what we can  actually see is that the long  single-stranded dna templates uh result  in partial integration  uh where the  five-prime junction uh relative to the  donor template  has these deletions occurring  and we do not see that with the  double-stranded dna template  all right  on this slide uh we're looking further  into that uh off target risk and so  again we have four different uh  templates for gfp nokians  um  and we're introducing that with a mock  off target double stranded break  with an rnp targeting the servancy one  locus  and so what we see is  when we deliver the unmodified templates  with this off-target  rnt we get up to  about 23  insertion at this off target site  if we add our modification in  uh we can bring that down to about eight  percent uh insertion  and what's nice is if we further treat  with this uh altar hdr enhancer v2 which  should modulate the repair pathways we  can actually bring this down to about  uh background detection levels  uh and now this is a worst case scenario  uh where the off-target double-stranded  dna break is being generated near uh 100  percent efficiency so i wouldn't expect  to see this high of levels with typical  cas9 off targets  all right uh so the last couple  experiments that i want to highlight  here are looking at improved designs  with the altar hdr donor blocks  uh so on this first slide we're looking  at the impact homology arm length with  varying size inserts uh and so we have  uh insertion sizes ranging from 120 base  pairs up to 2 kb  targeting different sites  and tested homology arm lengths  from 40 up to 300 base pairs for the  shorter sizes and then 40 up to 500 base  pairs for the longer inserts  and these were delivered as both equal  molar doses  where the differences are a little bit  more exaggerated as well as equal mass  and we actually looked at equal mass a  little bit  more uh since we found that cells are  limited on the total mass that can be  delivered before uh you're significantly  impacting cell viability  uh and so you can actually  increase the hdr rates a little bit more  for those shorter uh homology arms  but overall what you can see is that  the hdr8 begins plateauing around 100 to  200 base pairs  um  for the homology arm length for the  shorter inserts uh and then it begins  plateauing around 200 to 300 base pairs  for the longer  inserts  and so generally we recommend starting  out at 200 base pairs  as a default starting  point for your homology arm lengths  but this may vary by cell type or system  all right and so the last little bit of  data i have here is looking at  improvements uh in the design of  blocking mutations with larger inserts  this is very similar to the results that  we  observed with our single stranded algos  where if you're generating a 300 base  pair insert um  uh 20 base pairs upstream or downstream  of your cut site  um generally we found that  uh including blocking mutations that are  on the same side of your double-stranded  break as your insert uh is actually the  best design and so again with the pam  distal design uh we recommend uh using  those track mutations  um  and then with the pan distal mutation or  the pan proximal mutations excuse me  um a pain mutation alone is sufficient  all right uh so  overall we do recommend  careful consideration  of your donor template design  and the selection of your guide rna to  improve your hdr rates  from that aspect and our altar hdr  design tool  is an easy way to generate those  blocking mutations and look for  potential guide rnas  and we also recommend the use of our  ultra hdr enhancer v2 that just launched  this month  and that's been a game-changing way to  improve our hdr rates  and then finally the inclusion of  modifications in your donor templates  does allow you to stabilize those donors  and cells and promote your  hdr rates  so  in conclusion  i do want to  acknowledge our team  including our full  crispr r d group as well as our  bioinformatics scientists that support  us in all of our endeavors  our products and tools can be found on  the hdr and crispr websites  listed here i'm happy to take any  questions  and any additional inquiries can be sent  to our crispr idt dna.com email  all right
 

